[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Health Checks, Service Discovery, and API Gateways (YARP) in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern microservices architectures require robust patterns to ensure high availability, dynamic routing, and resilience. In the .NET ecosystem, YARP (Yet Another Reverse Proxy) serves as a high-performance API Gateway, while built-in Health Checks and Service Discovery abstractions provide the underlying foundations for monitoring and locating services."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Implementing Health Checks"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Health checks allow external orchestrators (like Kubernetes) to determine if your application is ready to accept traffic (Readiness) or if it needs to be restarted (Liveness). Below is how to configure basic health checks in Program.cs."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddHealthChecks()
    .AddCheck("self", () => HealthCheckResult.Healthy());

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapHealthChecks("/health");
app.Run();"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Integrating Service Discovery"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":".NET 8 introduces native service discovery abstractions, allowing your application to resolve service names to endpoints without hardcoding IP addresses."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"builder.Services.AddServiceDiscovery();

builder.Services.ConfigureHttpClientDefaults(http =>
{
    http.AddServiceDiscovery();
});"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Configuring YARP as the API Gateway"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"YARP is configured primarily via the appsettings.json file. It defines Routes (incoming requests) and Clusters (destination servers). YARP can integrate with .NET Service Discovery natively."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"{
  "ReverseProxy": {
    "Routes": {
      "catalog-route": {
        "ClusterId": "catalog-cluster",
        "Match": {
          "Path": "/catalog/{**catch-all}"
        },
        "Transforms": [
          "PathPattern": "/{**catch-all}"
        ]
      }
    },
    "Clusters": {
      "catalog-cluster": {
        "Destinations": {
          "destination1": {
            "Address": "http://catalog-service"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To wire up YARP in Program.cs, add the reverse proxy services to the dependency injection container and map the proxy endpoints."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddReverseProxy()
    .LoadFromConfig(builder.Configuration.GetSection("ReverseProxy"));

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapReverseProxy();

app.Run();"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always separate Liveness (is the app running?) from Readiness (can the app process traffic, e.g., database is up?)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use YARP active and passive health checks to automatically remove unhealthy destinations from the load-balancing pool."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage native .NET Service Discovery providers like Eureka, Consul, or configuration-based resolvers for cloud-native deployments."}]
